<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Iliad</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Iliad is an ancient Greek epic poem attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>, composed probably in the eighth or seventh century BCE. Set in the tenth year of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trojan-war">Trojan War</a>, it tells of the wrath of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achilles">Achilles</a> and its consequences, ending with the funeral of Hector. Its Catalogue of Ships names <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knossos">Knossos</a> first among the Cretan cities led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idomeneus">Idomeneus</a>, and the poem became the foundation of Greek education and literature.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/iliad.jpg" alt="Iliad" width="320" height="250" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Homer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Illiad_fragmented_papyrus.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
